the defect (measured in R3392, repaired here)
  preview/ugc-gate-report.html carries data-back-bytes = the size of
  preview/index.html (tools/face_backhop.py catch-all rule -> index.html),
  while index.html quotes that pair's sizes three ways (newest band,
  all-pairs band, registry table). Page size and pair bytes were each
  other's input; at the flip instant the recipe arm read
  pair-bytes-reproduced=False and only a second ring pass converged it.
  The same class already had a precedent on this page: CLOCK_WORD_WIDTH /
  CLOCK_PAD (R3298) pins a clock word to one width for exactly this reason.

what landed (one definition, all six sites)
  tools/deliverable_index.py: BYTES_WIDTH = 9 and BYTES_PAD(count) ->
  "%9dB" (whitespace collapses in HTML, the value and the rendered
  sentence are unchanged). Used by newest_line, the dry-run promise in
  render_newest, render_pairs, the registry table cell, and by the three
  arms that read those numbers back (AC-DIX2, AC-DIX13, AC-DIX21
  printed_html, and the AC-DIX23 dry promise). No literal %dB survives on
  a render path; the only remaining ones are detail strings and the
  stdout summary, which are not page bytes.
  src/os/test_deliverable_index.py: the three arm literals now compare
  against mod.BYTES_PAD(os.path.getsize(...)) instead of a second format
  string, so the criterion (quoted size == disk) is unchanged and the
  suite carries no private copy of the format.

